WEBVTT

00:00:00.666 --> 00:00:18.770
<v Casey>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got a volcano that may have quietly— no, that broke down its own methane pollution without anyone predicting it, an asteroid caught just six hours before impact, and the real question underneath both: what happens to a changing glacier if nobody's out there measuring it. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:32.245 --> 00:00:53.037
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Sunday, September 27th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Science, Wonder &amp; the Natural World.

00:00:54.037 --> 00:01:09.709
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're getting into a volcano that may have eaten some of its own methane pollution, an asteroid nobody saw coming until six hours before it burned up, and what it actually takes to notice something in this region before it's already changed.

00:01:10.419 --> 00:01:31.979
<v Casey>Quick weather check before we get into any of that. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's overcast, around 4 degrees, with a high of 15 and a low of 2 — no rain expected today. Around the region: 100 Mile House sitting near 2 with some cloud breaks, Williams Lake at 4 and overcast, Quesnel at 4 and mostly clear, Tatla Lake at 4 with partly cloudy skies.

00:01:32.586 --> 00:01:37.442
<v Riley>Winter tires are already in the news today, so keep that in your back pocket. Let's get into it.

00:01:38.842 --> 00:02:04.914
<v Casey>A quieter local news day, mostly close to home, with a couple of wrinkles worth the full story. Williams Lake's Tourism Discovery Centre just marked its 20th anniversary. The Williams Lake and District Chamber of Commerce's executive director spoke to it in a piece published Saturday — two decades of steering visitors toward the lake, the museum, the trails, and everything else that keeps people stopping instead of just driving through on Highway 97.

00:02:06.037 --> 00:02:12.469
<v Riley>Twenty years is a long run for a visitor centre in a town this size — most rural tourism outfits don't make it past a decade.

00:02:13.669 --> 00:02:31.733
<v Riley>Also in Williams Lake, and also Saturday, the Kings and Queens two-pitch tournament wrapped up, and by every account the weather held. Warm, dry, blue sky over the diamonds — the Tribune's writer called it perfect conditions, which for late September in the Cariboo is not something you can plan around.

00:02:32.933 --> 00:02:54.821
<v Casey>Less pleasant news for anyone with a road trip ahead. My Cariboo Now is reporting that winter tire requirements kick in on many northern B.C. highways starting next week. That's not a suggestion, it's regulation on specific stretches — and if your all-seasons are down to the wear bars, this is the week to deal with it, not the week you're already sliding through a corner on the Cariboo Highway.

00:02:56.021 --> 00:03:24.053
<v Casey>Down in Kelowna — outside our region, but the shape of the story will feel familiar to anyone who's sat through a Cariboo Regional District budget meeting — there's a push for the city to disclose the bids it received for managing Prospera Place, the arena downtown. Local reporting says a councillor and at least one bidder want the numbers made public before council signs off on the deal, arguing residents deserve to see what competing companies actually offered before their tax dollars are committed.

00:03:25.409 --> 00:03:32.713
<v Riley>Watch that one closely — every small town with a rec centre or arena contract eventually runs into this exact transparency fight.

00:03:33.912 --> 00:04:04.072
<v Riley>From municipal contracts into the bigger science stories, and this first one's been building for a couple weeks now. Tom's Hardware reported that data centers in the United States, built to run AI models, are projected to become the fifth-largest natural gas consumer in the entire world by 2035. The estimate is consumption growing by 15 billion cubic feet a day as compute demand keeps climbing — that puts data centers ahead of the total gas consumption of most countries on Earth.

00:04:04.589 --> 00:04:08.421
<v Casey>Fifth-largest consumer on the planet, and it's server farms, not a nation.

00:04:09.621 --> 00:04:36.797
<v Casey>Sticking with energy, but pointed the other direction — Popular Mechanics covered a study published earlier this month where researchers replicated the heat and pressure conditions found inside Western Australia's massive iron-ore deposits, and in doing so produced natural hydrogen gas in the lab. If that geological process holds up at the scale researchers think it might, there could be enormous untapped hydrogen reserves sitting underground already, no energy-intensive electrolysis required to make it.

00:04:38.116 --> 00:04:46.412
<v Riley>Natural hydrogen instead of the manufactured kind — you're not spending energy to make the fuel, you're just finding where the Earth already made it.

00:04:47.612 --> 00:05:12.500
<v Riley>NPR Health News ran a study on longevity that's worth the full read. Researchers found that short, vigorous bursts of movement matter almost as much for long-term health as total daily step count. You don't need an hour on a treadmill — a few minutes of hard effort scattered through the day, like sprinting for a bus or hauling something heavy fast, showed up as meaningfully protective in the data.

00:05:13.853 --> 00:05:19.661
<v Casey>Good news for anyone whose entire exercise routine is stacking firewood in bursts between coffee breaks.

00:05:20.861 --> 00:05:49.053
<v Casey>Less encouraging news out of Ohio. Science-Based Medicine reported on a fight over school vaccination policy, where anti-vaccine advocacy groups, including one founded by the current U.S. Secretary of Health and Human Services, are pushing back against a policy meant to exclude unvaccinated kids from school during active measles outbreaks. Measles is about as solved a public health problem as science has ever produced, and it's back in the news because of a policy fight, not a scientific one.

00:05:50.252 --> 00:06:11.836
<v Riley>Nieman Lab published a piece asking whether science journalism is dying, again, comparing today's shrinking science press to a similar collapse forty years ago. Back then, two of roughly seventeen popular science magazines in circulation folded in the same year, and commentators at the time called it a "science crash" — a sudden collapse, not a slow decline.

00:06:13.036 --> 00:06:31.116
<v Casey>New Atlas ran a piece debunking the idea that Friday the 13th is unusually deadly. Researchers looked at actual mortality data and found no meaningful spike tied to the date — the "deadliest days" pattern that does show up has nothing to do with superstition, and the myth has survived mostly because it's a satisfying story.

00:06:32.316 --> 00:06:58.124
<v Riley>Neuroscience News had one of the stranger findings this week. Researchers found that contracting your core muscles — during walking, lifting, even coughing — sends a pressure wave up the spinal column that triggers ultrafast constrictions in veins in the brain, actively regulating cerebral blood flow. This was published in PNAS, and nobody expected the abdominal muscles to be part of a brain circulation control system.

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:00.827
<v Casey>So core day turns out to be brain day too.

00:07:02.027 --> 00:07:22.435
<v Casey>ScienceDaily covered a 125-million-year-old crocodile fossil. Researchers shone ultraviolet light on the preserved skin and found sensory organs, sophisticated respiratory structures, and what looks like a distinct camouflage pattern on the tail — none of it visible under normal light, all of it sitting there in the fossil for well over a hundred million years.

00:07:23.808 --> 00:07:31.080
<v Riley>A pattern invisible for a hundred and twenty-five million years, until somebody thought to try a different wavelength of light on it.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:53.576
<v Riley>TechRadar reported on new research into tin-based solar cells that could trap heat a thousand times longer than current materials allow, potentially breaking past the 33 percent efficiency ceiling that's limited photovoltaic panels for decades. That number's been treated as close to a hard physical limit in solar engineering for a long time.

00:07:54.776 --> 00:08:15.480
<v Casey>ScienceDaily also had a piece on tropical trees that breathe at night. Some species evolved to take in carbon dioxide after dark specifically so they can keep their pores shut during the hottest, driest hours of the day, conserving water. Researchers compared genomes across species and traced how millions of years of genetic reshuffling produced that water-saving trick.

00:08:16.632 --> 00:08:23.808
<v Riley>That's the kind of mechanism that could matter for drought-stressed forests here too, if it ever translates to conifers.

00:08:25.007 --> 00:08:50.663
<v Casey>Last story before the spotlight — ScienceAlert covered a new paper in Royal Society Open Science where an Oxford mathematician argues the Parthenon's famous optical illusion might not be an intentional illusion at all. The long-standing claim is that ancient builders curved the columns deliberately to correct for how the eye perceives straight lines at that scale. This researcher says there's no real evidence the architects were solving that problem on purpose.

00:08:51.278 --> 00:08:55.214
<v Riley>Ha — a correction about a correction, twenty-five hundred years later.

00:08:56.114 --> 00:10:09.074
<v Casey>Speaking of corrections — we have one of our own to issue. In our September 26th episode, on Cariboo Local Affairs, we said we couldn't find candidate lists for Cariboo Regional District Electoral Areas D, E, and F, and we asked listeners to help us track down who was running in Area F specifically. We caught this on our end after the fact. The list had already been published by the Williams Lake Tribune on September 15th. Area D, covering Fox Mountain and McLeese Lake, has no vote at all — incumbent Steve Forseth was acclaimed. Area E, South Lakeside and Dog Creek, is contested — incumbent Melynda Neufeld faces challenger Mary Forbes. Area F, covering 150 Mile House, Horsefly, and Likely, also has no vote — incumbent Maureen LeBourdais was acclaimed there as well. We also said on that episode that we couldn't confirm which Williams Lake council candidate had withdrawn. That was Jared Wardlaw-Gimbel, who pulled out by the September 18th deadline, leaving fourteen names on the ballot. And we'd said eleven council candidates had no public record we could find at all — the Tribune had already reported that Paul French is a former city councillor, and that eight of the others are running for the first time. Those gaps were ours to close, not a gap in the public record.

00:10:10.319 --> 00:10:15.039
<v Riley>Good to get all three areas straightened out before nominations close on October 1st.

00:10:15.564 --> 00:10:17.932
<v Casey>Let's move to something a little gentler on the nerves.

00:10:19.332 --> 00:10:43.836
<v Riley>Scout Island Nature Centre in Williams Lake is run by volunteers with the Williams Lake Field Naturalists, and every fall they run the Salmon Trip out to Gavin Lake — three days of brood stock collection, stream surveys, and hands-on habitat research for students, the kind of fieldwork most kids never get within a hundred kilometres of otherwise. Applications are open now at scoutisland dot ca.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:48.680
<v Casey>Three days out at a stream counting fish teaches more than a semester of slides ever will.

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:57.868
<v Riley>And it feeds straight into what we're getting into next — who's actually out there watching these systems, and what happens to what they see once the field trip ends.

00:10:59.268 --> 00:11:25.132
<v Casey>Start with the volcano, because it's the strangest story on the list this week. Hunga Tonga erupted in January of 2022, threw an enormous plume of ash mixed with seawater straight into the stratosphere, and researchers now think sunlight hitting that mixture generated highly reactive chlorine, which in turn broke down some of the methane sitting up there in the atmosphere. Nobody predicted that reaction. Nobody was looking for it when it happened.

00:11:25.644 --> 00:11:42.932
<v Riley>They only caught it because satellites happened to be pointed at that stretch of sky at that moment, tracking the eruption for other reasons entirely. If the instrumentation coverage had been thinner that month, that whole chemical pathway just happens and disappears into the noise, unrecorded.

00:11:43.532 --> 00:11:58.260
<v Casey>Which is the actual question worth sitting with today. Something can happen, in full, physically, with real consequences for the atmosphere, and still never become knowledge unless the right sensor happened to be aimed at the right patch of sky at the right moment.

00:11:58.804 --> 00:12:14.652
<v Riley>Right, and the coverage is careful about it too — this is framed as a proposed mechanism, researchers say the theory is, not a confirmed climate fix. But it's real atmospheric chemistry, independently reported by more than one outlet, not a guess dressed up as a finding.

00:12:15.276 --> 00:12:31.428
<v Casey>It's real as an observation. It's also one data point tied to one unrepeatable, massive natural event. The distance between "we watched this happen once" and "we can build a climate lever out of it" is most of the actual scientific work still left to do, and none of that work has started.

00:12:32.083 --> 00:12:50.139
<v Riley>Nobody's proposing we go trigger eruptions to scrub methane, to be clear. The interesting part is the mechanism itself — stratospheric chlorine breaking down a potent short-lived pollutant. If that pathway is real, understanding it might inform other methane strategies even without replicating the volcano.

00:12:50.694 --> 00:13:11.590
<v Casey>Might. Deliberately injecting reactive chlorine into the stratosphere at any scale humans could control also carries its own ozone-depletion risk, which the coverage doesn't skip past lightly. This is a fascinating discovery. It is not a policy lever yet, and calling it something that "could reshape estimates" is doing exactly the speculative work a hedge is supposed to do.

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:34.690
<v Riley>Put the asteroid story next to it, because it's the same detection problem from a different direction. EarthSky reported a small asteroid hit the atmosphere near Australia recently, spotted only six hours before impact. It was harmless — it burned up — but it was also the thirteenth object caught this close to entry in recent tracking. That's becoming close to routine.

00:13:35.208 --> 00:14:06.208
<v Casey>Routine for the small, harmless ones. The asteroid that actually got attention this year, 2024 YR4, got years of tracking. Astronomers calculated an impact probability out to a decimal point — up to 1.2 percent for a 2032 pass at one stage — and it took months of refined observation before it was downgraded to no significant risk. Big and potentially dangerous gets a multi-year budget and constant retracking. Small and harmless gets six hours, and six hours turns out to be enough, because nobody has to evacuate anything.

00:14:06.847 --> 00:14:25.567
<v Riley>Which tells you something useful — detection windows in astronomy are designed around the size of the threat, not curiosity for its own sake. Survey networks are built and funded specifically to catch the rare large object years out. Everything small and harmless is basically a byproduct of a system built for something else.

00:14:26.167 --> 00:14:53.631
<v Casey>And that's the piece that actually lands close to home, once you take it off the sky and put it on the ground. We build monitoring for whatever's already been flagged as a threat with a clear name attached to it. Wildfire behaviour gets instruments and staff because everyone remembers the last fire season. Flood stage, avalanche risk, same story. A slow geological or hydrological shift with no single dramatic event attached to it almost never gets a network built around it at all.

00:14:54.244 --> 00:15:18.660
<v Riley>Glaciers in the Cariboo Mountains are the actual local example. Researchers have tracked mass balance on one of them for well over a decade — one of the longer continuous glacier records in this part of the province. It exists because a small number of people kept physically hiking up there and taking the same measurements year after year, not because there's a funding line guaranteeing it exists forever.

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:36.688
<v Casey>So the record survives entirely on personal continuity — the same fragility we've come back to before with salmon monitoring and with groundwater. If that specific research team stops making the trip, the glacier doesn't stop melting. It just stops being something anyone downstream can point to with numbers.

00:15:37.223 --> 00:16:02.247
<v Riley>It's the Hunga Tonga problem, scaled down and slowed down. The chemistry happened whether or not the satellite was watching. The ice loss happens whether or not anyone's walking up there with a GPS unit and a stake. What changes, in both cases, is whether anyone downstream — a water license holder, a fisheries biologist, a town planning its water supply twenty years out — ever gets to act on what happened.

00:16:02.819 --> 00:16:14.275
<v Casey>And unlike an eruption, glacier retreat doesn't announce itself with one dramatic afternoon. It's the kind of change you only see by holding this year's photo next to years of photos nobody else bothered to take.

00:16:14.775 --> 00:16:33.023
<v Riley>Which actually cuts against your usual argument. Normally you're the one telling me volunteer-based systems fail the moment the key person leaves. Here, the small-team research model is the only reason this particular record exists at all. There's no institutional guarantee behind it, and it still worked this long.

00:16:33.601 --> 00:16:58.537
<v Casey>It's not a contradiction, it's the same fragility seen from a different angle. The record exists, and it's valuable precisely because it's kept going this long. It's also one grant renewal, one retirement, or one researcher taking a different job away from a gap that nobody notices until water levels downstream start doing something nobody can explain, because the baseline that would have explained it stopped being collected years earlier.

00:16:59.071 --> 00:17:23.271
<v Riley>So the fix isn't scattering sensors across every creek and slope in the region hoping something useful turns up. It's picking a small number of physical processes here that actually matter to decisions — glacier mass, snowpack timing, groundwater level near a community well — and making sure at least one monitoring point on each has a home that outlives any single person's involvement in it.

00:17:23.954 --> 00:17:47.178
<v Casey>Who owns that point once the original research grant runs out is the real question, and it's not a funding question, it's a governance one. Not who pays for year one — who's still walking out there checking it in year eight, and whether that responsibility sits with a university department three hundred kilometres away, a regional district, or a nation whose territory the glacier or the creek actually sits on.

00:17:47.833 --> 00:18:17.969
<v Riley>There's a working version of exactly that already running in the region, just built around a different resource. One Cariboo First Nation has, as publicly reported, been tracking river flow and water temperature for salmon management purposes for years. That data collection didn't start as a climate monitoring project. It functions as one anyway, because flow and temperature are precisely the baseline you'd want if you were trying to understand how a watershed is changing over decades.

00:18:18.635 --> 00:18:39.403
<v Casey>That's the model to point to, then, more than a new pilot with a press conference and a two-year funding clock attached. An existing, working, locally-held dataset keeps running because it answers a question the community actually needs answered every single season — not because a grant officer found it exciting one particular year and stopped finding it exciting the next.

00:18:40.012 --> 00:18:58.772
<v Riley>Which means the honest answer to whether a place exists if nobody's recording it is that it exists either way. The glacier keeps melting whether or not anyone's up there with a stake and a camera. What doesn't survive the gap is anyone's ability to act on what's happening to it before it's already happened.

00:18:59.326 --> 00:19:12.446
<v Casey>And that gap doesn't close by wishing for more sensors scattered around the landscape. It closes when whoever's doing the watching has a reason to keep watching that has nothing to do with whether this year's grant cycle finds the project fundable.

00:19:13.046 --> 00:19:45.662
<v Riley>Next time a regional district, a school science program, or a local naturalist group is looking for a project, ask whether there's already one physical marker near your own community being measured by somebody — a stream gauge, a snow stake, a long-running local record — that could simply keep being measured, instead of starting something new from zero and hoping it survives past the pilot phase. And if anyone listening knows of one, we'd like to hear about it — corrections, tips, anything at all, feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:19:46.305 --> 00:19:59.025
<v Casey>Find the thing that's already running before building something new, and ask who inherits it when the person currently doing it moves on. We'll take that tip over another volcano story any day of the week. Have a good one out there.

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:00.778
<v Riley>Have a great rest of your day.